    "evidence_text": "Detecting\"Smart\"Spammers On Social Network: A Topic Model Approach\tSpammer detection on social network is a challenging problem.\n\n\"simple\" spammers, whose tweets contain only malicious links, those \"smart\" spammers are more difficult to distinguish from legitimate users via content-based features alone  .\n\nThere is a considerable amount of previous work on spammer detection on social platforms.\n\nResearcher from Twitter Inc.\n\nSome researchers focus on the clustering of urls in tweets and network graph of social spammers  ,  ,  ,  , showing the power of social relationship features.As for content information modeling,  apply improved sparse learning methods.\n\nThe two features contain both local and global information, and the combination of them can distinguish human-like spammers effectively.\n\nObservation\tAfter exploring the homepages of a substantial number of spammers, we have two observations.\n\n1) social spammers can be divided into two categories.\n\nSpammers and legitimate users show different interests on some topics e.g. commercial, weather, etc. To better illustrate our observation, Figure.\n\nTherefore we can detect spammers by means of the difference of their topic distribution patterns.\n\nThe spammers are all purchased commercially from multiple vendors on the Internet.\n\nRecall is the ratio of spammers that are detected so.",
